Machine learning models are increasingly used across trading, lending, and recommendation systems, with many firms continuously retraining models on live market data. As more companies adopt and fine-tune the same foundation models, these systems are no longer learning independently. Each firm's retraining changes market behavior, which then becomes the training data for every other firm.

Today, models are certified individually. If a single model's retraining process converges, it is considered safe. However, this ignores interactions between firms. In reality, firms continuously influence one another through the data they generate, allowing small disturbances to compound over time.

To address this, we derive a closed-form market-level stability condition based on a single quantity called the \textbf{feedback reproduction number}, $m_N$. The market is stable if and only if $m_N < 1$, providing a way to measure the stability of the entire market rather than each model in isolation.

Our analysis shows that instability is driven more by \textbf{shared models} than by the number of firms. Fifty firms fine-tuning the same foundation model behave like one learner fifty times larger. We also show that slowing retraining only works below a critical crowding threshold, while feedback-corrected learning and greater model diversity provide complementary ways to restore stability. Finally, we reproduce the \textbf{1.74$\times$} and \textbf{3.16$\times$} amplification observed in a real multi-dealer order-flow market. Together, these results shift AI risk assessment from certifying individual models to monitoring a single market-wide stability metric, giving firms and regulators practical tools to identify and prevent instability before it emerges.
